<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Prince Marko</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1335–<a href="/years/1395">1395</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Kraljević Marko, Marko Mrnjavčević</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Prince Marko, Marko Mrnjavčević, was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vukasin-mrnjavcevic">King Vukašin</a> and ruled a realm around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prilep">Prilep</a> in Macedonia after his father's death at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-maritsa">Battle of the Maritsa</a> in 1371. He became an Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> and was killed fighting for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mircea-the-elder">Mircea the Elder of Wallachia</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-rovine">Battle of Rovine</a> in 1395. As Kraljević Marko he is the best-loved hero of South Slavic epic poetry.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/prince-marko.jpg" alt="Prince Marko" width="320" height="481" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marko_Mrnjavcevic.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Marko was born about 1335, the first son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vukasin-mrnjavcevic">Vukašin Mrnjavčević</a> and his wife Alena. The family name comes from Mrnjava, whom the Ragusan historian Mavro Orbin described as a minor nobleman from Zachlumia. The earliest written reference to Vukašin, from 1350, calls him the appointed governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prilep">Prilep</a> for Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-dusan">Stefan Dušan</a>. In July 1361 Dušan's successor Uroš appointed Marko to head an embassy to Ragusa to negotiate peace. Peace was not reached, but Marko secured the release of detained Serbian merchants from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prizren">Prizren</a> and was allowed to withdraw <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> his family had deposited in the city. That account holds the earliest undisputed reference to him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Vukašin, co-ruler with Uroš, crowned Marko young king in 1370 or 1371. On 26 September 1371 Vukašin was killed and his forces defeated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-maritsa">Battle of Maritsa</a>, and when Uroš died in December 1371 Marko formally became king of the Serbian land. The Serbian nobles, who had become effectively independent, did not recognise him.</p><p>By 1372 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durad-i-balsic">Đurađ I Balšić</a> had taken <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prizren">Prizren</a> and Peć and Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazar-of-serbia">Lazar Hrebeljanović</a> had taken <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pristina">Pristina</a>, and by 1377 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vuk-brankovic">Vuk Branković</a> held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skopje">Skopje</a>. Marko kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prilep">Prilep</a> and ruled a small territory in western Macedonia, sharing power with his brother Andrijaš. He funded the Monastery of Saint Demetrius near Skopje, completed in 1376 and known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/markos-monastery">Marko's Monastery</a>. He became an Ottoman vassal, though the date is uncertain and probably not immediately after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maritsa">Maritsa</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Marko died on 17 May 1395 fighting against the Wallachians at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-rovine">Battle of Rovine</a>. Although a ruler of modest historical significance, he became a major figure of South Slavic oral tradition, venerated as a national hero by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, Macedonians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a> as a protector of the weak who confronted the Turks.</p><p>In Serbian epic poetry he is Kraljević Marko, prince or king's son, and the poems, composed by anonymous poets under Ottoman rule, bind together adventures that show his character. Bulgarian and Macedonian tales say a vila raised him and gave him supernatural strength. Legends make him a giant who created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demir-kapija">Demir Kapija</a> gorge with a stroke of his sabre. Dramas, paintings and sculptures, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-mestrovic">Ivan Meštrović</a>'s Marko on Šarac, took up the figure.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Prince Marko” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The emperors were Stefan Dušan (1346–1355) and Stefan Uroš V (1355–1371); Vukašin of Serbia was co-ruler with the title of king from 1365, and <b>Prince Marko</b> was designated heir as young king from 1369.</blockquote><a href="/empire/serbian-empire">Serbian Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>After Vukašin was killed at the Maritsa in 1371, his son <b>Marko</b> inherited the royal title and a much smaller realm around Prilep, Ohrid and the Pelagonian plain.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-prilep">Kingdom of Prilep</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Livno, in what is now <a href="/countries/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a>. Died in Lower Danubian Plain. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.83,17.01&amp;year=1374" aria-label="Livno on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4958 611 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Prince Marko"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5408 881h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5408 881h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.83,17.01&amp;year=1374">What was here in 1374? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
